    "trigger_card_number": 489,
    "title": "LESSON 24: THE HIGH VERBS",
    "header_color": "magenta",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 1,
        "content": [
          "Every verb in this cluster has a plain",
          "English twin that says the same thing.",
          "The Norman word says it in a room where",
          "the register has risen.",
          "",
          "  commence    beside begin",
          "  purchase    beside buy",
          "  conceal     beside hide",
          "  abandon     beside forsake",
          "",
          "Nothing is added but weight. You buy a",
          "sandwich and purchase a house, and the",
          "act is the same act.",
          "",
          "THE NEXT 20 CARDS: verbs that outrank",
          "their English twins."
        ],
        "pronunciation": "Lesson 24"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "025",
    "trigger_word": "reply",
    "trigger_card_number": 509,
    "title": "LESSON 25: THE NARROWER VERB",
    "header_color": "magenta",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 1,
        "content": [
          "More verbs with English twins. Here the",
          "Norman word does not only lift the",
          "register: it narrows the sense.",
          "",
          "  reply       beside answer",
          "  devour      beside eat",
          "  perish      beside die",
          "  seize       beside grab",
          "",
          "Everyone dies; crops and armies perish.",
          "Anyone can eat; a starving man devours.",
          "The English twin is the wider word; the",
          "Norman one arrives with a condition.",
          "",
          "THE NEXT 20 CARDS: verbs that narrow",
          "what the plain twin says."
        ],
        "pronunciation": "Lesson 25"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "026",
    "trigger_word": "courageous",
    "trigger_card_number": 529,
    "title": "LESSON 26: HIGH ADJECTIVES",
    "header_color": "magenta",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 1,
        "content": [
          "Adjectives now. The Germanic twin is the",
          "word a child learns first; the Norman",
          "one is the word an adult writes down.",
          "",
          "  courageous  beside bold",
          "  profound    beside deep",
          "  feeble      beside weak",
          "  tranquil    beside still",
          "",
          "A deep well and a profound silence take",
          "the same measurement. Only the room has",
          "changed.",
          "",
          "THE NEXT 25 CARDS: French adjectives",
          "and the plain words beneath them."
        ],
        "pronunciation": "Lesson 26"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "027",
    "trigger_word": "companion",
    "trigger_card_number": 554,
    "title": "LESSON 27: NAMES FOR PEOPLE",
    "header_color": "magenta",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 1,
        "content": [
          "The Normans renamed people by their",
          "place in a household and a rank.",
          "English kept its plainer names beside",
          "the new ones.",
          "",
          "  companion   beside friend",
          "  peasant     beside farmer",
          "  spouse      beside mate",
          "  traitor     beside turncoat",
          "",
          "Companion is com plus panis: one you",
          "share bread with. Friend says as much",
          "in half the breath.",
          "",
          "THE NEXT 20 CARDS: people under their",
          "French names."
        ],
        "pronunciation": "Lesson 27"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "028",
    "trigger_word": "liberty",
    "trigger_card_number": 574,
    "title": "LESSON 28: THE ABSTRACT NOUN",
    "header_color": "magenta",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 1,
        "content": [
          "Abstract nouns. French built them with",
          "-ty and -ity; Old English built them",
          "with -ness, -ship and -dom. English",
          "kept both sets.",
          "",
          "  liberty     beside freedom",
          "  amity       beside friendship",
          "  felicity    beside happiness",
          "  frailty     beside weakness",
          "",
          "The endings are the tell. A state noun",
          "ending in -ty came in from French. The",
          "-ness ending was already here.",
          "",
          "THE NEXT 20 CARDS: states of mind in",
          "their French names."
        ],
        "pronunciation": "Lesson 28"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "029",
    "trigger_word": "enmity",
    "trigger_card_number": 594,
    "title": "LESSON 29: FEELING AND STATE",
    "header_color": "magenta",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 1,
        "content": [
          "More abstractions, and a second French",
          "ending: the -or nouns, which name a",
          "quality as though it were a substance.",
          "",
          "  enmity      beside hatred",
          "  vigour       beside strength",
          "  rancour      beside bitterness",
          "  poverty     beside need",
          "",
          "Chivalry is chevalerie, what a man on a",
          "horse owed; homage is what a man owed",
          "his lord. The words came with the system.",
          "",
          "THE NEXT 20 CARDS: what people feel,",
          "in French."
        ],
        "pronunciation": "Lesson 29"
      }
    ]
  },
